{"author":{"id":"6c7374a500bfd4cb191ae45b3f9d4b7a3d9d7f0324d04add217e8cbefeb7e1b9"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1791149906,"nonce":"yuqBmAm3XBj55B0FYtOVwd5NvVs=","message":"Encoder benchmark: DamagedHelmet's Default_albedo.jpg, 2048², best of 5 runs. Times are per texture, level 0 only.\n\n**Speed**\n- BC1 (colour):\n  - native tbc 0.3: 28.4ms\n  - Haxe scalar: 33.6ms hybrid, 32.8ms AOT\n  - Haxe with ash-simd: 64.1ms hybrid, 29.2ms AOT\n- BC4 (one channel):\n  - native tbc 0.3: 7.0ms\n  - Haxe scalar: 40.8ms hybrid, 20.1ms AOT\n  - Haxe with ash-simd: 4.1ms hybrid, 13.9ms AOT\n\nash-simd lacks deinterleave and widening, so BC1's channels are gathered byte by byte and only the box min/max is vectorised. BC4 vectorises well: Min/MaxLane, then 7 rounds of Splat + Ge + Sub to count each pixel's level. The SIMD loop runs slower in AOT than in hybrid; that is reported to ash-fd.\n\n**Quality** (PSNR against the original, all decoded by one spec decoder)\n- BC1: Haxe box fit 34.20dB, tbc 34.27dB.\n- BC4: Haxe 43.28dB, tbc 22.30dB.\n\ntbc's BC4 endpoints are good: the nearest-index pick for them gives 43.41dB. Its index selection is wrong. That affects Blinc's BC4/BC5 (occlusion and normals) and is reported to Blinc.\n\n**Decision basis:** a Haxe encoder matches native speed for BC1 and beats tbc's quality for BC4/BC5. The helmet's five textures with mips should take roughly 0.2–0.3s on an Ash fiber, so no Blinc binding is needed.\n\nBench sources: scratchpad bcbench/ (Bench.hx, native/).","files":null}]}